DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"cr-homer-bandit-cave","L":"treasure","n":"Train robbers’ money in a Homer cave","la":44.02135,"lo":-91.55653,"y":"1900-1903","pl":"Bluff caves near Homer, six miles below Winona","co":"Winona","stt":"MN","v":"Train-robbery money (\"$?,000\", amount unreadable)","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"In 1900 strangers came to Homer, six miles down the river from Winona, looking for money said to have been hidden in one of the caves near Homer by bandits who had held up a train. The man who knew the place was said to be in prison for life and to have told them where it was. Nothing was found. In October 1903 a Homer resident announced a systematic search. The amount is OCR-damaged (\"$?,000\"). Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Look for small sandstone caves and rock shelters in the bluffs behind Homer. Most of the bluff land is private or state forest; caves can hold bats (closed in winter). Permission: caves and bluffs, permission and bat rules.","src":[["\"Homer's Hidden Treasure,\" Minneapolis Journal, Oct. 22, 1903 (Winona dateline)","https://www.loc.gov/collections/chronicling-america/?q=%22hidden+money%22&fa=location_state:minnesota&dates=1903-10-22/1903-10-22"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-rice-bank-loot-1904","L":"treasure","n":"Rice bank robbers’ cache, woods back of Richmond","la":45.45413,"lo":-94.51833,"y":"1904","pl":"Woods back of Richmond, Stearns County","co":"Stearns","stt":"MN","v":"Rest of the December 1904 Rice bank loot","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"After the bank at Rice (Benton Co.) was robbed in December 1904, Charles F. Morgan was taken off a Great Northern freight at Paynesville with two of the bank's money sacks ($397.95), and George Myers and Charles Young were arrested at Richmond. The sheriffs of Stearns, Benton and Morrison counties believed the men had a cache in the woods back of Richmond, and townspeople went hunting for the hidden money. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Check later papers for whether the rest of the Rice loot was recovered; if not, the woods and old road edges just outside 1904 Richmond are the search ground. Permission: private land.","src":[["Brainerd Daily Dispatch, Dec. 16, 1904","https://archive.org/details/brainerd-daily-dispatch-1904-12-16"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-franconia-mill-1864","L":"history","n":"Ansell Smith’s steam sawmill, Franconia","la":45.37024,"lo":-92.6916,"y":"1850s-1864","pl":"Mill Reserve on the St. Croix, Franconia","co":"Chisago","stt":"MN","v":"Coins, tokens, mill tools and boom hardware of the 1850s-60s","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"town","st":"On June 29, 1864 Chisago County sheriff J. J. Mosher seized the lands of Ansell Smith to satisfy an execution and offered them at the Register of Deeds office in Taylors Falls on Aug. 20, 1864: parts of lot 1 sec. 10 and lot 9 sec. 2 T33N R19W outside the Franconia plat, the NE1/4 SE1/4 sec. 18, and two-fifths of the lot called the \"Mill Reserve\" in the town of Franconia, \"together with the steam saw mill, booms and fixtures thereon\".","t":"The mill stood on the St. Croix at Franconia; look for the mill reserve on the Franconia plat and for slab, sawdust and boom-pier remains along the bank. Lumber-town sites give coins, tokens and tools. Permission: check ownership; river bank.","src":[["Sheriff's sale notice, Taylors Falls Reporter, July-Sept. 1864","https://archive.org/details/sep10186401tayl"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the Franconia mill reserve on the St. Croix; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-garrison-village-sauk","L":"ghost","n":"Garrison, deserted mill and ferry village","la":43.4966,"lo":-89.6307,"y":"about 1848-1860s","pl":"Lower narrows of the Baraboo River","co":"Sauk","stt":"WI","v":"1850s coins, buttons, potsherds and kiln waste","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Garrison was a deserted village site near the lower narrows of the Baraboo River, named for Andrew Garrison, who built a mill and settled there about 1848. Mrs. Ann Garrison made the place famous by \"salting\" a mine, building a pottery and keeping a ferry across the river, and she was postmaster in 1858. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"About 3 hours from Eau Claire. A mill, ferry landing and pottery give a small, early (1850s) site: look on the 1859 or 1877 Sauk County maps and the GLO plat for the mill dam below the narrows. Potsherds and kiln waste would mark the pottery. Permission: check ownership; river bank.","src":[["Baraboo Weekly News, June 13, 1912 (list of old Sauk County post offices)","https://www.loc.gov/collections/chronicling-america/?q=%22deserted+village%22&fa=location_state:wisconsin&dates=1912-06-13/1912-06-13"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-schultz-recluse-1914","L":"treasure","n":"Harris Schultz’s buried money","la":45.515,"lo":-93.44974,"y":"1914","pl":"Near his home in Spencer Brook township, near Cambridge","co":"Isanti","stt":"MN","v":"\"Several thousand dollars\"","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Harris Schultz (age given as \"12\", probably 72) lived alone in Springbrook [Spencer Brook] township near Cambridge and was found dead in September 1914. He was afraid to keep his money in banks, and it was supposed that several thousand dollars were buried somewhere near his home. His only known relative was a sister in Minneapolis, and a search was to be made. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Needs the farm: an Isanti County plat (about 1914) under \"Schultz\" or \"Schulz\", or the probate file at the county court. Check later papers for whether the money was found. Permission: private land.","src":[["\"Search for Buried Money,\" Askov American, Sept. 24, 1914","https://www.loc.gov/collections/chronicling-america/?q=%22buried+money%22&fa=location_state:minnesota&dates=1914-09-24/1914-09-24"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-snyde-hermit-cf-1900","L":"treasure","n":"Cooper Snyde, hermit furrier’s money","la":44.9369,"lo":-91.3929,"y":"1899-1900","pl":"His house or shop in Chippewa Falls (not yet placed)","co":"Chippewa","stt":"WI","v":"About $400 found in 1900; \"a large amount still concealed\"","c":"newspaper","s":"partly","p":"town","st":"Cooper Snyde, an old furrier who lived as a hermit, died in December 1899. In January 1900 a 15-year-old boy, John Seesiger, sorting old boxes of fur clippings and rags, found currency from $1 to $1,000 bills and a box of small gold and silver pieces, about $400. \"There is believed to be a large amount still concealed somewhere.\" Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Find his shop or house on the Chippewa Falls fire maps (1883-1890s) or city directory; a furrier's yard and outbuildings are where the rest would be. Permission: town lots, ask owners.","src":[["\"A Hermit's Wealth Found,\" Duluth Evening Herald, Jan. 1900 (Chippewa Falls dateline)","https://archive.org/details/jan1190003dulu"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-hudson-peterson-gold-1936","L":"treasure","n":"Hans Peterson’s plowed-up gold coins","la":44.97469,"lo":-92.75687,"y":"1936","pl":"A field near Hudson (farm not yet placed)","co":"St. Croix","stt":"WI","v":"Six gold coins of 1834-1850s found; the rest of a broken container may remain","c":"documented","s":"partly","p":"approx","st":"In September 1936 Hans Peterson plowed up six gold coins dated about 1834 to the 1850s, worth $100, near Hudson. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"A plowed-up scatter usually means a broken container: the rest may lie in the same field. Needs the farm: the 1897 St. Croix plat (Rumsey) or a 1930s plat under \"Hans Peterson\". Permission: private farmland.","src":[["\"Gold Coin Finds Reported in Press,\" The Numismatist 49 no. 9 (Sept. 1936) p. 705; Kleeberg 2009 no. 729","https://archive.org/details/numismaticfindso00klee"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-wabeno-bank-loot-1921","L":"treasure","n":"Wabeno State Bank loot, buried in the woods","la":45.424,"lo":-88.16,"y":"1921","pl":"Woods near Wabeno; a 1922 search moved to a logging trail west of Athelstane","co":"Forest","stt":"WI","v":"$32,000 in cash and securities","c":"newspaper","s":"never","p":"approx","st":"Three men robbed the Wabeno State Bank of $32,000 in cash and negotiable securities on July 16, 1921. Willis and Lawrence Lesperance and Chalmer Yaeger were caught at Chippewa Falls in August and got 25 years each at Crandon. By their confession they buried the loot in the woods some distance from Wabeno while posses were on their trail. In July 1922 Lawrence was brought from Waupun for a day-long search that found nothing. In August 1922 berry pickers found a weather-beaten touring car on an abandoned logging trail in the wilderness about 20 miles east of Wabeno, several miles west of Athelstane, and the search moved there. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Edge of the 4-hour range (about 275 km). Check later Appleton or Green Bay papers for whether the loot ever turned up. Much of this country is county and national forest: follow forest rules. Permission: public forest rules, detecting may need a permit.","src":[["Appleton Post-Crescent, July 18-29, 1921, July 15, 1922 and Aug. 8, 1922; Stevens Point Gazette, Aug. 24, 1921; Janesville Daily Gazette, Sept. 22, 1921","https://archive.org/details/appleton-post-crescent-1922-08-08"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-lemuel-hill-hoard-1906","L":"treasure","n":"Lemuel Hill’s miser hoard, Pleasant Hill township","la":43.89038,"lo":-91.54044,"y":"1906","pl":"His farm in Pleasant Hill township (section not yet placed)","co":"Winona","stt":"MN","v":"About $50,000 found in 1906; \"considerably still to be unearthed\"","c":"newspaper","s":"partly","p":"approx","st":"Lemuel Hill, an aged bachelor and recluse of Pleasant Hill township, died in a Winona hospital in October 1906. On his deathbed he named a man who knew where his valuables were, but the man knew only vaguely. After an all-day dig, searchers found notes, mortgages, certificates of deposit and other property worth about $50,000 under the barn floor, and several thousand dollars in cash and securities buried in the wheat in the granary. He left no will. In January 1907 \"it is believed that there is considerably still to be unearthed\". Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Classic miser farm: barn, granary and the ground around them where he shovelled out spoiled grain for years. Needs the farm's section from an old Winona County plat or the probate file. Permission: private farmland.","src":[["\"Hoard of Miser Dug Up on Farm,\" Minneapolis Journal, Oct. 26, 1906; Little Falls Herald, Nov. 2, 1906; \"Buried Treasure,\" Kanabec County Times, Jan. 11, 1907","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn83045366/1906-10-26/ed-1/?sp=1"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]},{"id":"cr-progress-ghost-town-wood","L":"ghost","n":"Progress, ghost town in the town of Rock","la":44.55563,"lo":-90.25697,"y":"before 1937","pl":"Town of Rock, Wood County (not on the Rock page of the Wood plat book)","co":"Wood","stt":"WI","v":"Early settlement coins and buttons","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"In January 1937 the Wisconsin Rapids Daily Tribune called Progress, in the town of Rock, a \"ghost town\", in the obituary of Mrs. Christina Anderson, one of Wood County's oldest pioneers, who was \"identified from its beginnings\" with it. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Needs placing: the Wood County plat book (research/plat-wood) or an old post-office list should show Progress. Only about 1 h 45 min from Eau Claire. Permission: private land.","src":[["Wisconsin Rapids Daily Tribune, Jan. 21, 1937","https://archive.org/details/wisconsin-rapids-daily-tribune-1937-01-21"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: the town or township named in the article; not yet checked on a plat or aerial."]}]);
(function(F){for(const p of DATA.points){const f=F[p.id];if(!f)continue;for(const k in f){if(k==='src0')p.src=[f.src0,...(p.src||[]).map(s=>[s[0]+' (later retelling)',s[1]])];else if(k.endsWith('+')){const b=k.slice(0,-1);if(b==='src')p.src=[...(p.src||[]),f[k]];else p[b]=(p[b]||'')+f[k];}else p[k]=f[k];}}})({"t-tr-green-isle":{"n":"Curran brothers’ cellar hoard, Green Isle Township","la":44.67369,"lo":-94.07195,"pl":"Curran family farm, Green Isle Township (approximate)","y":"1946","v":"Over $200,000 in silver dollars, 1,162 gold coins and bonds (found)","c":"documented","s":"found","p":"approx","pinconf":"C","st":"The Curran family farmed 300 acres in Green Isle Township from before the Civil War to 1946 and distrusted banks. After the last brother, Daniel, died on May 18, 1946, bank president James Mullen and Gus McPartlan probed the cellar floor with muskrat spears and found milk pails of silver dollars, 1,162 gold coins in Union Leader tobacco cans, and bonds under the stairs and between the floor joists: over $200,000 in all. The Probate Court ordered the gold ($14,235 face) sold to James and Meta Lee of Chicago for $29,865. A later retelling turned this into outlaw brothers burying $40,000 of bank loot at the north end of Mud Lake; no source for that version has turned up. Pin is approximate (the farm is placed only to the township).","t":"The cellar was searched thoroughly in 1946, so what is left is the farmstead ground: yard, outbuildings and lanes. The lesson for other sites: frugal bachelor farmers who distrusted banks hid money in the cellar floor and between the joists. Permission: private farmland, ask the owner.","src0":["Stafford King, \"Gold in Curran Hoard Totalled $14,235,\" The Numismatist, May 1952, pp. 474-476; Kleeberg, Numismatic Finds of the Americas (2009) no. 870","https://archive.org/details/Numismatist1952May"]},"t-fort-crawford-payroll":{"st+":" A documented robbery here is better ground than the legend: in the 1830s two discharged soldiers dug under a corner picket and took three $5,000 kegs of silver. One keg burst by the picket, one on the Mississippi bank, where all but about $600 was recovered, and the third was found months later two miles below the fort by a man spearing fish by torchlight. The men were tried and let go. The lost $600 lay in the sand of the 1830s river bank, which has since moved: check old maps.","t+":" Permission: city/park land and river bank, permission needed.","src+":["William H. C. Folsom, Fifty Years in the Northwest (1888), pp. 13-14, \"Fort Crawford Robbed\"","https://archive.org/details/fiftyyearsinnort00folsuoft"]}});
