// Treasure-web leads (v185): 3 new tw- pins from old newspapers (Donnerstag 1908, Maas 1898, Vollmer 1899)
DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"tw-donnerstag-gold-1908","L":"treasure","n":"Donnerstag brothers' $4,000 in gold coin, Manson Lake woods","la":45.5625,"lo":-89.6329,"y":"1908","pl":"Woods around the old Donnerstag home near Manson Lake, about 8 miles west of Rhinelander (pin on Manson Lake)","co":"Oneida","stt":"WI","v":"$4,000 in gold coin said to be hidden in the woods (may have been their own counterfeit coin)","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Hugo, Albert and Rudolph Donnerstag were arrested near Rhinelander in August 1908 for making counterfeit $5 gold pieces and silver; agents found $1,035 in spurious gold coin, plaster molds, lathes and a plating outfit, and 11 guns in the house. The mother tried to get away in a boat, so the home was on a lake; Hugo and Albert's place was about 18 miles out and Rudolph lived nearer the city. On Oct. 25 the three sawed a bar and went down a rope from the Dane County jail. They went straight home, and the Madison papers (Nov. 5) said it was because a pot of gold, $4,000 in gold coin, was secreted in the woods and they feared forest fires would destroy it. Hugo and Albert gave up at Rudolph's house about twelve miles from Rhinelander; Rudolph was shot in both legs while running through the woods near Manson Lake, eight miles west of the city, near the old Donnerstag home. None of the papers read says the $4,000 was dug up, and since they were coiners it may have been their own make. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Look for the 1908-14 Oneida County plat book or tax roll to find the Donnerstag land (Manson / Woodboro area, town of Woodboro or Newbold), then old cellar holes and the woods edge around it. Any gold coins of 1900-08 from there should be weighed and rung: counterfeits were cast and plated. Permission: private or county forest land, ask first.","src":[["Neb's clipping (Madison, Nov. 5 [1908]); Vilas County News, Oct. 28, Nov. 4 and Nov. 11, 1908; Eagle River Review, Nov. 6 and Nov. 13, 1908; Ladysmith News-Budget, Aug. 13 and Nov. 5, 1908; Vernon County Censor, Aug. 19, 1908","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85040614/1908-11-13/ed-1/?sp=6"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed on Manson Lake (GNIS 45.5625, -89.6329) from 'near Manson lake, eight miles west' (Eagle River Review, Nov. 13, 1908); the house itself is not yet placed."]},{"id":"tw-maas-pot-of-gold-1898","L":"treasure","n":"Heinrich Maas' pot of gold, Cooperstown","la":44.3128,"lo":-87.7745,"y":"1898","pl":"The Maas place near Cooperstown, town of Cooperstown (farm not yet placed)","co":"Manitowoc","stt":"WI","v":"A buried pot of gold named in his will (OCR reads about $1,500)","c":"newspaper","s":"unknown","p":"town","st":"Heinrich Maas of Cooperstown left a will that disposed of a pot of gold he had buried; its place was written on a slip of paper kept in his watch case. When the estate looked, the hole the pot had sat in was found, but the gold was gone. In March 1898 the probate court cited his son William Maas to tell what he knew about it. The papers read do not say it was ever found. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Edge of the 4-hour range. An 1893-95 Manitowoc County plat would show the Maas farm; the empty hole means someone moved it, possibly not far. Permission: private farm land, ask first.","src":[["Manitowoc Pilot, Feb. 17 and Mar. 17, 1898","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn85033139/1898-03-17/ed-1/?sp=3"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: GNIS Cooperstown; farm not yet checked on a plat."]},{"id":"tw-vollmer-hoard-1899","L":"treasure","n":"George Vollmer's money in the walls, Stillwater","la":45.0564,"lo":-92.806,"y":"1899","pl":"Vollmer house in Stillwater (address not yet placed)","co":"Washington","stt":"MN","v":"$7,000 to $10,000 found hidden in cracks and crevices","c":"newspaper","s":"found","p":"town","st":"George M. Vollmer of Stillwater, supposed to be rich, died in the fall of 1899 and little property could be found. His two sons, who had been cut off, searched the house and found money of all kinds hidden in cracks and crevices everywhere, an estimated $7,000 to $10,000. He had always considered banks unsafe, and the search was to go on until every article in the house was examined. Pin is approximate (town or township only), not the spot itself.","t":"Found in 1899, so this is a lesson more than a target: bank-shy townsmen hid money in the house itself, in walls and cracks, not in the yard. A Stillwater city directory of the 1890s would give the address; if the house still stands, only the owner can look. Permission: private property.","src":[["Grand Rapids (Minn.) Herald-Review, Nov. 18, 1899 (Stillwater, Nov. 14 dispatch)","https://www.loc.gov/resource/sn82002441/1899-11-18/ed-1/?sp=3"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the source text only: GNIS Stillwater city point."]}]);

