DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"wd2-02","L":"ghost","n":"Phipps (railroad hamlet) (1883-1907)","la":46.06278,"lo":-91.41361,"y":"1883-1907","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"topo","st":"A railroad hamlet with a post office from 1883 to 1907. It was named for W. H. Phipps, a railroad official. Pin: The era USGS topo draws it at the pin (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Hayward 1971 topo: \"Phipps\" label and buildings at pin; GNIS none","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. Only a Wikipedia stub. Check a 1900s plat for the depot and store sites.","src":[["en.wikipedia.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phipps,_Wisconsin"]],"pinconf":"A","pinev":["USGS Hayward 1971 topo: \"Phipps\" label and buildings at pin; GNIS none"]},{"id":"wd2-03","L":"ghost","n":"Draper and Loretta (Hines lumber hamlets) (1892-1935)","la":45.88639,"lo":-90.83111,"y":"1892-1935","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"topo","st":"Lumberman Edward Hines named Loretta in 1892 for his wife and daughter. Draper's post office ran from 1906 to 1935. Both were Hines lumber company settlements in the Flambeau cutover. Pin: The era USGS topo draws it at the pin (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Loretta 1971 topo: Draper village drawn ~100 m NW of pin on WI-70","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. Loretta coordinates: 45.88556, -90.85306.","src":[["en.wikipedia.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draper_(community),_Wisconsin"]],"pinconf":"A","pinev":["USGS Loretta 1971 topo: Draper village drawn ~100 m NW of pin on WI-70"]},{"id":"wd2-04","L":"ghost","n":"Yarnell (1915-1933)","la":45.72694,"lo":-91.41944,"y":"1915-1933","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"topo","st":"A post office hamlet from 1915 to 1933, named for the Yarnell family. Pin: The era USGS topo draws it at the pin (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Edgewater 1972 topo: \"Yarnell\" label and buildings at pin","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. Only a Wikipedia stub. Find the store and post office on a 1920s plat.","src":[["en.wikipedia.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yarnell,_Wisconsin"]],"pinconf":"A","pinev":["USGS Edgewater 1972 topo: \"Yarnell\" label and buildings at pin"]},{"id":"wd2-08","L":"camp","n":"McCord's lumber camp, Somo Lake (c.1875-1885)","la":45.50374,"lo":-89.84694,"y":"c.1875-1885","pl":"Lincoln County","co":"Lincoln","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1910s coins, tools, camp hardware","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A major camp of Myron McCord, congressman and owner of the Lincoln County Advocate. He went bankrupt after the Bradleys built Tomahawk, and McKinley later made him territorial governor of Arizona. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Bradley 1971: sec 22 on Somo Lake shore, no camp drawn (gone by then); section matches source","t":"Camps sat near water and the tote road; the cook shack and bunkhouse spots are the best ground. The coordinates are the center of the section, from the BLM PLSS lookup.","src":[["www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org","https://www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org/custom/sturdevantchandler/lumberCamps.html"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Bradley 1971: sec 22 on Somo Lake shore, no camp drawn (gone by then); section matches source"]},{"id":"wd2-09","L":"camp","n":"Leahy's lumber camp (c.1880s)","la":45.63644,"lo":-89.91047,"y":"c.1880s","pl":"Lincoln County","co":"Lincoln","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1910s coins, tools, camp hardware","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A camp probably run by John E. Leahy, a Civil War veteran (35th Wis.) and Wausau mayor and state senator. Crews and supplies came in over the tote road from the Worcester depot. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Pier Lake 1971: sec 6, no feature at pin; section matches 1883 map cited","t":"Camps sat near water and the tote road; the cook shack and bunkhouse spots are the best ground. Section center, from the BLM PLSS lookup.","src":[["www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org","https://www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org/custom/sturdevantchandler/lumberCamps.html"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Pier Lake 1971: sec 6, no feature at pin; section matches 1883 map cited"]},{"id":"wd2-10","L":"camp","n":"Leahy's Little Rice camp (c.1880s)","la":45.59181,"lo":-89.84803,"y":"c.1880s","pl":"Lincoln County","co":"Lincoln","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1910s coins, tools, camp hardware","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Leahy's second camp. It banked logs in winter and drove them downstream at spring breakup. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Bradley 1971: sec 22 by Little Rice River marsh, no feature; section matches","t":"Camps sat near water and the tote road; the cook shack and bunkhouse spots are the best ground. Section center, from the BLM PLSS lookup.","src":[["www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org","https://www.tomahawkhistoricalsoc.org/custom/sturdevantchandler/lumberCamps.html"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Bradley 1971: sec 22 by Little Rice River marsh, no feature; section matches"]},{"id":"wd2-11","L":"camp","n":"Old logging camp, east shore of Cranberry Lake (Chippewa Flowage) (early 1870s-)","la":45.92205,"lo":-91.10516,"y":"early 1870s-","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1910s coins, tools, camp hardware","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A logging camp from the early 1870s. Ramona Cabins was built nearby in 1926 and later became Lovetere's Landing, which closed around 1972. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Barker Lake 1972: on dry ground at Cranberry Lake east shore, no feature","t":"Camps sat near water and the tote road; the cook shack and bunkhouse spots are the best ground. GPS from Dettloff's history-tour POI csv. The shoreline may be LCO reservation or USFS land, so check ownership. A 1910 map of the camp and Barrett's store is on the same site.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Barker Lake 1972: on dry ground at Cranberry Lake east shore, no feature"]},{"id":"wd2-12","L":"gather","n":"Crane Lake logging operation and Adolph Lessard stopping place (1889-1894, then stopping place)","la":45.95934,"lo":-91.27022,"y":"1889-1894, then stopping place","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1920s coins, tokens, buttons","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A major logging operation ran here from 1889 to 1894. Afterward the site became a stopping place run by Adolph Lessard. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Chief Lake 1971: dry ground SE of Crane Lake, clearing, no building","t":"Stopping places fed and bedded teamsters and river crews: work the yard, the barn site and the road approach. Dettloff GPS. Check whether the point is dry ground or flowage edge.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Chief Lake 1971: dry ground SE of Crane Lake, clearing, no building"]},{"id":"wd2-13","L":"gather","n":"Lessard's stopping place, farm and hotel (first flooded) (c.1880s-1923)","la":45.89376,"lo":-91.08897,"y":"c.1880s-1923","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1870s-1920s coins, tokens, buttons","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A stopping place, farm and hotel that had become a sportsmen's resort by 1900, where guests like William Wrigley stayed. It was the first property flooded when the flowage filled in 1923. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Barker Lake 1972: dry ground by spot height 1384 above flowage shore; source says upstream of Winter dam","t":"Stopping places fed and bedded teamsters and river crews: work the yard, the barn site and the road approach. Mostly underwater. A low-water drawdown shoreline could be worth a look.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Barker Lake 1972: dry ground by spot height 1384 above flowage shore; source says upstream of Winter dam"]},{"id":"wd2-16","L":"gather","n":"Berger's Eagle Lodge Resort (1904-1916)","la":45.91362,"lo":-91.28748,"y":"1904-1916","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1900s-1910s coins, jewelry, fishing gear","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"John Berger's Eagle Lodge was a full fishing and hunting resort on the Chief River from 1904 to 1916. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Chief Lake 1971: dry peninsula on Chief River arm; no building drawn","t":"Work the lodge footprint, the cabin rows, the dock landing and the paths between them. A resort that was gone before the flowage was built. Good for a resort-era coin spill.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Chief Lake 1971: dry peninsula on Chief River arm; no building drawn"]},{"id":"wd2-18","L":"history","n":"Thad Thayer's original trading post (1865) (1865-1882)","la":45.90674,"lo":-91.14759,"y":"1865-1882","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1860s-1880s trade goods, coins, buttons","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Thad Thayer was the first white settler at Pahquahwong. He built his trading post and home here in 1865 and left after 1882, when the land was allotted to Mary Miller. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS New Post 1972: on island hill 1363 with trail, building nearby; boat access only","t":"Trade-era ground: record what you find, and leave anything Native American in place. REFERENCE ONLY. LCO reservation, near the Old Post graveyards. Do not detect without tribal permission.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS New Post 1972: on island hill 1363 with trail, building nearby; boat access only"]},{"id":"wd2-19","L":"history","n":"Johnnie Moore (Claude Maddox) gangster hideout (1938-)","la":45.94849,"lo":-91.12559,"y":"1938-","pl":"Sawyer County","co":"Sawyer","stt":"WI","v":"1930s coins, cartridges, hardware","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A place built in 1938 by Claude Maddox (alias Johnnie Moore), a Capone enforcer suspected in the St. Valentine's Day Massacre. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS New Post 1972: east-shore point, sec 16, buildings nearby; 1938 hideout","t":"A story stop first; the ground is private, so ask the owner. Gangster-era lore. Buried-cash talk often attaches to these places, but this source gives no cash story. Check whether the building still stands as a private home.","src":[["www.chippewaflowage.com","https://www.chippewaflowage.com/four-dozen-points-of-historical-interest-on-a-two-hour-tour-of-the-east-side-of-the-lake-by-john-dettloff/"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS New Post 1972: east-shore point, sec 16, buildings nearby; 1938 hideout"]},{"id":"wd2-29","L":"ghost","n":"Buswell lumber town (burned 1910) (1905-1910)","la":46.17632,"lo":-89.80372,"y":"1905-1910","pl":"Vilas County","co":"Vilas","stt":"WI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A Milwaukee Road sawmill town of several hundred people, wiped out by a 1910 forest fire. The coordinates are for the boat landing, not the townsite. Pin: Placed from the source's GPS point only; not seen on the era topo (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Winchester 1955: on Papoose Lake SW shore by road; no town drawn (burned 1910)","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. The town footprint is on private property. Ask the owners. About 3.5 h from Eau Claire.","src":[["theclio.com","https://theclio.com/entry/203361"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["USGS Winchester 1955: on Papoose Lake SW shore by road; no town drawn (burned 1910)"]},{"id":"wd2-39","L":"ghost","n":"Lewiston (Sciota Twp) stage town (1851-1866)","la":44.50694,"lo":-93.08056,"y":"1851-1866","pl":"Dakota County","co":"Dakota","stt":"MN","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"A stop on the Northfield-Hastings road that faded after the railroads bypassed it following the 1857 panic. Gov. Edward Thye and Justice Pierce Butler came from here. Pin: Placed from GNIS, an address or an offset feature (about 100-400 m) (source GPS). Cross-check: GNIS Lewiston Post Office + Railroad Station (historical) within 1 m; USGS Farmington 1957 buildings at junction","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. Use the schoolhouse as the anchor. The coin-spill zones are the stage stop and the stores.","src":[["en.wikipedia.org","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewiston,_Dakota_County,_Minnesota"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["GNIS Lewiston Post Office + Railroad Station (historical) within 1 m; USGS Farmington 1957 buildings at junction"]},{"id":"wd2-43","L":"ghost","n":"Matchwood (Diamond Match camps town) (1888-c.1918)","la":46.55616,"lo":-89.39641,"y":"1888-c.1918","pl":"Ontonagon County","co":"Ontonagon","stt":"MI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"topo","st":"The Diamond Match Co. founded it in 1888 to serve its logging camps. It had over 200 people and many businesses for about 30 years, then turned to dairy farms by WWI. Pin: The era USGS topo draws it at the pin (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Matchwood 1956: Matchwood label + buildings on rail at pin; GNIS Matchwood Station 6 m","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. Nothing of the old town remains, so plats are needed to find business lots.","src":[["www.ghosttowns.com","https://www.ghosttowns.com/states/mi/matchwood.html"]],"pinconf":"A","pinev":["USGS Matchwood 1956: Matchwood label + buildings on rail at pin; GNIS Matchwood Station 6 m"]},{"id":"wd2-48","L":"ghost","n":"Mansfield mine town (1893 flood disaster) (1880s-c.1918)","la":46.1191,"lo":-88.2196,"y":"1880s-c.1918","pl":"Iron County","co":"Iron","stt":"MI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"In 1893 the Michigamme River broke into the Mansfield Mine and killed 27 miners. The mine was sealed and the town had emptied by WWI. Remnants of log buildings survive, and a small museum commemorates the disaster. Pin: Placed from GNIS, an address or an offset feature (about 100-400 m) (GNIS). Cross-check: Moved to GNIS \"Mansfield Location\" (mine settlement, Lake Mary quad); source point 5 km NE at Michigamme Mtn road; atlas \"Mansfield\" pin is the later P.O. 3.8 km SE","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. The coordinates are for the township. Find the townsite on a plat. The mine area is a disaster site, so be respectful.","src":[["www.ghosttowns.com","https://www.ghosttowns.com/states/mi/mansfield.html"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Moved to GNIS \"Mansfield Location\" (mine settlement, Lake Mary quad); source point 5 km NE at Michigamme Mtn road; atlas \"Mansfield\" pin is the later P.O. 3.8 km SE"]},{"id":"wd2-51","L":"ghost","n":"Redridge stamp-mill town and steel dam (1905-1960s)","la":47.15048,"lo":-88.76235,"y":"1905-1960s","pl":"Houghton County","co":"Houghton","stt":"MI","v":"1850s-1930s coins, tokens, buttons, relics","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"topo","st":"An Atlantic Mining Co. stamp-mill town with over 500 people by WWI. It became a ghost town by the late 1960s. The church, old hotel and a few homes remain. Pin: The era USGS topo draws it at the pin (source GPS). Cross-check: USGS Beacon Hill 1954: Redridge label, houses and Redridge Dam at pin","t":"Work the house rows, the store and depot sites and the paths between them; check who owns the ground now and ask first. The steel dam is a landmark. Ask before detecting the hotel and house lots.","src":[["www.ghosttowns.com","https://www.ghosttowns.com/states/mi/redridge.html"]],"pinconf":"A","pinev":["USGS Beacon Hill 1954: Redridge label, houses and Redridge Dam at pin"]}]);
{ const F={"ab4-chengwatana-village": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: An Ojibwe village that became the Pine County seat in 1856 (called Alhambra, then Chengwatana). It was a frontier military post in 1862-63 and had Elam Greeley's toll dam at the Cross Lake outlet. It lost the county seat to Pine City in 1870, and the post office closed in 1873. Its placement: 45.83861, -92.93750 (source GPS; USGS Pine City 1961: Snake River rapids E of Pine City; GNIS Chengwatana marker 1.1 km W; pin on river edge).", "src": ["en.wikipedia.org", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chengwatana,_Minnesota"]}, "fr-whitewater-falls-village": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: An abandoned townsite with a post office from 1856 to 1899. Legislator Clark W. Trisler lived there. Its placement: 44.12056, -92.00361 (source GPS; USGS Plainview 1934: road junction with school + X718 at pin; GNIS PO has no coordinates).", "src": ["en.wikipedia.org", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitewater_Falls,_Minnesota"]}, "fr-frankford-village-1856": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: A frontier village with 3 stores, a school, 3 blacksmith and wagon shops, a gristmill, a chair and coffin factory, and the Patchin Hotel, which held the post office. It was named county seat but lost to Austin, then died when the railroad passed 4 mi south in 1870. Its placement: 43.7138, -92.4582 (GNIS; Moved 2.9 km E to GNIS Frankford Cemetery (sec 24, Deer Creek, far east Frankford Twp as source says); source point was township centre).", "src": ["en.wikipedia.org", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankford_Township,_Mower_County,_Minnesota"]}, "fr-clear-grit-flouring-mill-hamlet-carrollton": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: A mill town on the South Branch Root River. Post office 1878-1882, with 107 people in 1880. Its placement: 43.70889, -92.03222 (source GPS; USGS Preston 1965: bridge BR 888 on South Branch Root River at pin, buildings across road).", "src": ["en.wikipedia.org", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clear_Grit,_Minnesota"]}, "fr-pequaming-old-indian-village-site-and-heba": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: The Hebard-Thurber sawmill of 1878 was the largest on Lake Superior. Henry Ford bought the town in 1923 and ran it as a model town until the mill closed in 1942. It had parks, churches, bath houses and an Odd Fellows hall. Its placement: 46.85187, -88.40041 (source GPS; USGS Keweenaw Bay 1954: Pequaming town drawn at pin).", "src": ["beaumier.nmu.edu", "https://beaumier.nmu.edu/ghosttowns/towns/Pequaming"]}, "fr-ramsay-and-the-eureka-mine": {"add": " Web dive 2026-10-30: An iron mining town with an abandoned high school, an abandoned downtown and a big stone-arch railroad trestle. Its placement: 46.47206, -89.99743 (source GPS; USGS Wakefield 1955: Ramsay village drawn at pin, mine dumps N).", "src": ["www.ghosttowns.com", "https://www.ghosttowns.com/states/mi/ramsay.html"]}, "t-neillsville-jar": {"add": " The farm was the Marg Fur Food Ranch (owner Kurt Marg, there 50 years), so the jar was older than his time on it; earlier owners who hid one jar may have hidden others. Find the Marg farm on a 1960s Clark County plat.", "src": ["Clark County history: 1962 silver jar", "https://wiclarkcountyhistory.org/3data/73/73109.htm"]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=F[p.id]; if(!x) continue; p.st=(p.st||"")+x.add; if(x.src) (p.src=p.src||[]).push(x.src); } }

