// County-depth card notes: chippewa-1913-enrich.json
{ const E={"ob-chippewa-city": {"st": " The 1913 county history says the village claimed 500 people at its height and had a store or two, a blacksmith shop, the O'Neil (later Stanley) sawmill, a district school and a tri-weekly post office, with F. G. Stanley still postmaster in the 1880s: 'Chippewa City is no more.' It also names three paper towns platted in Eagle Point in the 1856 boom, Eliside, Dell City and Manidowish, each with its own state bank. Lots were sold, but they were farmland by 1913 and their sites are not given.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "lm-12": {"st": " The mill here is the Gravel Island mill. Ephraim E. Shaw and Martin Daniels began it in 1857, and Bussy & Taylor finished it in 1858 with booms above the island. It burned in 1863, was rebuilt by James A. Taylor, and was run by the French Lumber Co. The 1913 history calls Gravel Island 'a small settlement' (Randall 1875 and Chippewa County 1913).", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"], ["Randall, History of the Chippewa Valley (1875)", "https://archive.org/details/historyofchippew00rand"]]}, "pc88-eagle-point-p-o-sec-6-t29n-r8w": {"st": " The 1913 county history: Eagle Point, about seven miles north of Chippewa Falls, was one of the pioneer settlements, with a post office, a mill run for some years by L. J. Wiltse and John Mitchell's cheese factory (the 1888 plat's cheese factory in sec. 6). It had about fifty people.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pb13-edson-center": {"st": " The 1913 county history: Edson Chubb, the first settler (1857), built a steam sawmill in 1860 and platted the south part of sec. 6 as a village, which 'has not even to this day materialized'. E. X. Adams kept the store here in 1873-84. About half a mile away, at a spot called Frog Town, Harrison C. Chubb built a water and steam flour and grist mill in 1883. The German Catholic church (Sacred Heart) dates from about 1870 and seated 500.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pc88-morris-craft-p-o": {"st": " By 1913 the county history lists Morris, with Irvine, as a former Wisconsin Central station that 'modern maps do not indicate': a station that came and went in the 1880s-1900s.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pc88-vale-p-o": {"st": " The 1913 county history: the hamlet of Vale on Hay Creek dates from the early days of the town of Auburn and had a post office, two churches, a school and stores. It had about fifty people at its best.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pc88-cox": {"st": " The 1913 county history calls Cox a former small lumber settlement on the Yellow River in the town of Colburn. The McElmurry brothers ran a mill there, as well as a post office and store. By 1913 only the crossroads of Huron was left as a hamlet in the town.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pc88-bobs-creek-stock-and-dairy-farm-sec-18-t": {"st": " The first town election of Cleveland (then Birch), in April 1885, was held at 'Stickney's Stopping Place', and the Stickneys were among the town's earliest settlers (1913 county history). This Stickney farm on the Cleveland side of the river was probably that stopping place, a teamsters' stop on the road up the Chippewa, though the book does not give its section.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "pc88-lake-house-on-long-lake-sec-18-t32n-r8w": {"st": " By 1913 Long Lake had become 'a very popular resort' for Chippewa Falls people and visitors from as far as Chicago, and 'the hotels are taxed to their utmost during the summer months' (1913 county history), so this shore saw resort crowds from the 1880s on.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}, "d-chippewa-fairgrounds": {"st": " The 1913 county history: a county agricultural society organized in December 1877 held its fair 'for many years' on these grounds before the Northern Wisconsin State Fair Association took over about 1896. On the big day of 1912, 27,000 people were on the grounds.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin, vol. 1 (1913)", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }
