// FIELDNOTESEC: T27N R9W field notes (1849): the upper Eau Claire mill on the chained line

// County-depth card notes: clark-1918-enrich.json
{ const E={"pc-yolo": {"st": " The 1918 county history says Yolo, about a mile west of Chili on the Omaha line, \"was formerly the location of a number of large charcoal pits,\" and the lumbering chapter lists \"Yolo near Chili\" with Thorp, Curtiss, Colby and Unity as charcoal-pit sites making fuel for the iron furnaces. Charcoal pits leave round flat pads of black soil 8 to 12 m across; look for them on LiDAR and in a plowed field.", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "pc-mill-york-sec-24": {"st": " This is the Romadka mill. In 1883 the Romadka brothers brought an old sawmill from Seymour and rebuilt it on the NW 1/4 of the NW 1/4 of sec. 24, York, making wagon hubs, spokes and felloes; settlers who had trouble getting paid called the place \"Fizzlerville.\" In 1890 the Milwaukee Road and the Omaha raced to the crossing near here, with an engine chained to the track and armed men guarding it (1918 history, pp. 663, 695-696).", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "ab3-mapleworks": {"st": " C. H. Ide (1918 history, p. 695) puts Maple Works \"where Sections 1, 2, 11 and 12 cornered, in the town of Grant\"; mail for the town of York was left there for years. Nelson Marsh, the postmaster, was also justice of the peace, mended shoes, pulled teeth and fiddled for all the dances. Another page calls it half a mile northeast of Granton, so check both sides of that section corner.", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "ab4-huntzicker-tavern": {"st": " In December 1864 a special election for county treasurer was held at Henry Huntzicker's house on the tote road. One candidate served free drinks, free meals at all hours and a free dance, and the precinct of 22 voters returned 101 votes for him (1918 history, p. 700). Settlers drove ox teams to George's and Henry's to dance till daylight (p. 699).", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "d-ghost-kurth": {"st": " Charles Kurth, who came in 1873 and owned 140 acres in sec. 21 and 40 in sec. 22 of Grant, built a large twelve-room brick tavern in 1882 at the place \"ever since known as Kurth's Corners,\" and town meetings were held there for a time (1918 history, biography). That farm is about 2.4 km south of this pin, near the sec. 21-22 corner; check there too.", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "ab3-westons-rapids": {"st": " Emma Robinson, who came in January 1859, remembered a dam and bridge across the river, a sawmill and grist mill, a tavern for the lumbermen and several tenement houses, and kept the first post office there. By 1860 it had a store, a furniture shop, a large public house and about 260 people. The plat was on lots 7 and 8 and the NE 1/4 of the SE 1/4 of sec. 2 (pp. 113, 662, 706). The 1918 book also places one of the four 1840s Mormon logging camps near Weston's Rapids (p. 36).", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "ab4-wedges-creek-taverns": {"st": " The 1918 history (p. 36) names the \"Mormon Riffle below the mouth of Wedge's Creek\" as one of four camps where Mormon loggers cut timber in 1844-46 for the Nauvoo temple. In 1854 the camps were grown up in wild plum, with the remains of mud-chinked log cabins and root-cellar holes, and \"broken crockery has at various times been unearthed at all four.\" The riffle is on the mile of river between this creek mouth and the Dells dam.", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "ab3-hemlock-dam": {"st": " A. S. Armstrong, foreman of the Hemlock dam for 13 years, said they ran about a million feet of logs an hour through it in season. He kept a store and post office at Hemlock for two years, and the 1914 flood took out the dam, the grist mill and the sawmill (1918 history, p. 698).", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}, "pc-longwood": {"st": " Amanda Smith kept a loggers' hotel at Longwood from about 1880 to 1900; the first one burned and they reopened over the Longwood store. Longwood was a stop on the 1890 stage line from Neillsville through Christie, Greenwood and Hemlock to Withee (1918 history, pp. 651, 682).", "src": [["History of Clark County, Wisconsin (Curtiss-Wedge, 1918), read cover to cover", "https://archive.org/details/fl-12689268-322470"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }

