// County-depth card notes: pierce-1937-enrich.json
{ const E={"pp77-mill-at-esdaile-sec-28-t25n-r17w": {"st": " Lowater's History of Pierce County (1937): James Buckingham built the first dam and sawmill here, about six miles up the Isabelle from Lake Pepin. In the 1870s the Chas. Betcher Co. of Red Wing took the mill over, raised the dam and added a spoke and stave factory, cutting logs from Hartland, Ellsworth, Salem, El Paso, Rock Elm and Union. A village of several hundred workmen grew up, with churches, hotels, stores, a school, a creamery, a furniture factory and blacksmith shops. The post office opened in 1869 under Hiram Patch. After the timber was gone the company pulled out, and by 1937 'scarcely a trace of it' was left: the village ground around the mill is the target.", "src": [["History of Pierce County, Wisconsin, for use in the common schools (Chas. Lowater, Spring Valley, 1937)", "https://archive.org/details/historyofpiercec00lowa"]]}, "pp77-village-of-warren-sec-7-t24n-r16w": {"st": " Lowater's History of Pierce County (1937): when Saratoga (the Phillips Bros. steam sawmill settlement of 1853 at what became Bay City) was abandoned, nearly all its buildings were hauled to Warrentown on the ice one winter.", "src": [["History of Pierce County, Wisconsin, for use in the common schools (Chas. Lowater, Spring Valley, 1937)", "https://archive.org/details/historyofpiercec00lowa"]]}, "t-plum-creek-diamonds": {"st": " Lowater's History of Pierce County (1937) gives the earlier story: the Hawn family, who ran the Rock Elm sawmill from the late 1860s, washed gold along Plum Creek with George H. Nichols of Prescott and found 'considerable gold and two diamonds' in the creek sand, but not enough to pay for the work. W. W. Newell, a western miner drawn to Rock Elm by the odd geology, later hunted the 'mother lode' and in 1890 turned up the iron ore deposits that led to the Spring Valley iron furnace.", "src": [["History of Pierce County, Wisconsin, for use in the common schools (Chas. Lowater, Spring Valley, 1937)", "https://archive.org/details/historyofpiercec00lowa"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }

// County-depth card notes: marathon-1913-enrich.json
{ const E={"pm80-knowlton-village-plat-sec-28-t26n-r7e": {"st": " History of Marathon County (Marchetti, 1913): Knowlton was a pinery settlement that grew up around one roadhouse and the Stark family's sawmill a short way below it. Leonhard Guenther, who came up in 1849 and worked in the mills and camps, bought the tavern in the early 1850s. As the Knowlton House it was the most popular stopping place between Wausau and Stevens Point, until the Wisconsin Valley Railroad reached Wausau in 1874 and ended the road travel. In 1913 the tavern still stood, with a large hall beside it that was the center for gatherings on festival days. That means the tavern, its hall and the road through them are the best ground here, where they lie above the Du Bay water line.", "src": [["History of Marathon County, Wisconsin, and Representative Citizens (Louis Marchetti, 1913), Town of Knowlton", "https://archive.org/details/historyofmaratho00marc"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }

// County-depth card notes: chippewa-1913-pass2-enrich.json
{ const E={"pc88-bobs-creek-stock-and-dairy-farm-sec-18-t": {"st": " Second source, Past and Present of Chippewa County (Forrester, 1914), Town of Cleveland: when Cleveland (first named Birch) was split from Eagle Point in January 1885, the first town election was held in April 1885 at \"Stickney's Stopping Place.\" Leonard Stickney's place here was a travelers' stop as well as a stock farm, and on that election day the whole town voted in his house or yard, so the old house and barn sites are worth more than plain farm ground.", "src": [["Past and Present of Chippewa County, Wisconsin (Thomas J. Forrester, 1914), Town of Cleveland", "https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }

// County-depth new pins: monroe-1912-new-pins.json
DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"cd-cataract-stage-stop","L":"history","n":"Cataract: stage-line stop with two hotels","la":44.0877,"lo":-90.8424,"y":"1850s-1880s","pl":"Cataract, Town of Little Falls (GNIS village point; 1897 plat village by the mill pond, sec. 27 T19N R4W)","co":"Monroe","stt":"WI","v":"Coins 1850s-1880s, stage and freighter losses, harness and hotel items","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"History of Monroe County (1912): when Sparta was the railhead, freight was teamed through Little Falls township both ways, between Sparta and the lumber woods and Black River Falls. A daily stage ran between Cataract and Sparta and on to Black River Falls, and another line ran from Sparta and Cataract to Eau Claire. The township became a center for travelers' stopping places, with six hotels at one time: two in the village of Cataract and the others at Max Craig's, Sam Bacon's, Hugh Gallagher's and Pett's tavern. The 1897 plat shows the village and the mill pond.","t":"Find the two hotel lots on the 1897 plat page, then the old stage road through the village and the dam crossing. Ask first.","src":[["History of Monroe County, Wisconsin (Richards, 1912), township histories","https://archive.org/details/historyofmonroec00richrich"],["Plat Book of Monroe County, Wisconsin (1897), Little Falls page","https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/record/maps/W0174X1/x"],["USGS GNIS","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["The pin is the GNIS Cataract point, 0.5 km from the 1897 mill pond pin (pmo97-cataract-mill-pond-sec-27-t19n-r4w). The hotel lots are not placed."]},{"id":"cd-leon-west-tavern","L":"history","n":"Leon: D. M. West's early tavern","la":43.8775,"lo":-90.8301,"y":"1850s","pl":"Leon, Town of Leon (GNIS village point)","co":"Monroe","stt":"WI","v":"Coins 1850s on, tavern and travel losses","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"History of Monroe County (1912): in the early days D. M. West kept a tavern at Leon. He wanted the town named Miranda after his wife, but a settler named Lawton proposed Leon, after a valley he had seen in Mexico, and the name stuck. Settlers came in from 1851 (Peter DeCoursey, the Wakemans, Kudneys and Carriers), and Charles J. Matteson ran a store from 1854. No other pin is within 1 km of the village.","t":"The tavern lot is not fixed. Start at the old crossroads and the mill, and use a 1938 air photo to find the old buildings. Ask first.","src":[["History of Monroe County, Wisconsin (Richards, 1912), township histories","https://archive.org/details/historyofmonroec00richrich"],["USGS GNIS","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["The 1912 history names the tavern at Leon; the pin is the GNIS Leon point. The building is not placed."]}]);

