// River landings (research/river-landings)
DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"rl-rumseys-landing","L":"history","n":"Rumsey's Landing: Chippewa River steamboat stop and winter harbor (1861-1880s)","la":44.735,"lo":-91.7,"y":"1861-1880s","co":"Dunn","stt":"WI","p":"approx","pl":"Chippewa River about 2 miles below Caryville, near the old mouth of Mud Creek","v":"Steamboat-landing losses 1860s-80s: Seated coins, tokens, buttons, boat and wagon hardware","st":"History of Northern Wisconsin (1881) puts Rumsey's (also printed Ramsey's) Landing on the Chippewa 'fifteen miles below Eau Claire', where John Rumsey ran a store, steamboats and a mill from 1861 to 1874, and names it beside the mouth of Mud Creek, the 1825 treaty boundary point (the 1914 Eau Claire history repeats this). A post office opened there in 1877. A Wisconsin Historical Society photo (about 1895) places the landing about two miles below Caryville. The steamers Chippewa and Johnny Smoker, both owned by H. S. Rumsey of La Crosse, burned in winter quarters here on January 21, 1871.","t":"Find the landing and the store site on the 1888 Dunn plat (Rock Creek and Peru pages) or the earliest topo, then work the high bank above flood level and the road down to the river. Ask the owner first.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881)","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"],["History of Eau Claire County (Bailey, 1914)","https://archive.org/details/historyofeauclai00bail"],["Wisconsin Historical Society image IM39353 (Rumsey's Landing, about 1895)","https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/Records/Image/IM39353"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["Two kinds of source (county histories; a captioned photo) agree on the name and on 'about 2 miles below Caryville', but no map fixes the spot yet","pin is an estimate on the river, good to about 1.5 km"]},{"id":"rl-wright-woodyard-1848","L":"history","n":"Madison Wright's steamboat wood yard on the Mississippi (1848)","la":44.40045,"lo":-92.04442,"y":"1848-1850s","co":"Buffalo","stt":"WI","p":"plss","pl":"Sec. 11, T22N R14W, Town of Nelson, on the bank of the Mississippi","v":"Early coins, buttons, axe heads and chain from a wood yard that fed steamboats","st":"History of Northern Wisconsin (1881), Buffalo County chapter: 'In the same year [1848], Madison Wright opened a wood yard on Section 11, Town 22, Range 14, on the bank of the Mississippi, in what is now the town of Nelson.' It is one of the first white businesses in the county, the same year Probst and Wecker claimed the Alma site.","t":"Steamboats tied up at wood yards to load cordwood, and crews and woodcutters camped there. Find the bank within section 11 that a boat could reach (the main channel side, above flood level). Much of the bottomland is Upper Mississippi Refuge or state land: check the Land box first.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881)","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the centre of section 11; the yard was on the river bank inside it"]},{"id":"rl-britts-landing","L":"history","n":"Britt's Landing: Mississippi steamboat landing (1850s)","la":43.61546,"lo":-91.21898,"y":"by 1854-1880s","co":"Vernon","stt":"WI","p":"plss","pl":"Sec. 9, T13N R7W, between Warner's Landing and Bad Ax City (Genoa)","v":"1850s-80s coins, tokens and steamboat hardware","st":"History of Vernon County (1884): 'Bad Ax City, located on section 28, township 13, range 7; Britt's Landing, on section 9; and Warner's Landing on section 4 ... all contain good landings on the Mississippi.' Merrick's steamboat memoir (1909) says the packet Nominee 'carried the broom until she sank at Britt's Landing, below La Crosse, in 1854.'","t":"Look for the old landing road down the bluff in section 9 on LiDAR and the earliest topo; the bank itself may be railroad grade or refuge.","src":[["History of Vernon County (Springer, 1884)","https://archive.org/details/historyofvernonc00spri"],["Merrick, Old Times on the Upper Mississippi (1909)","https://archive.org/details/oldtimesonupperm00merr"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the centre of section 9"]},{"id":"rl-cole-ferry-zumbro","L":"history","n":"Isaac Cole's Zumbro River ferry and hotel (1851)","la":44.31595,"lo":-91.9999,"y":"1851-1850s","co":"Wabasha (MN)","stt":"MN","p":"plss","pl":"Sec. 22, Greenfield Township (T110N R10W), south bank of the Zumbro","v":"1850s coins, buttons, harness and tavern losses","st":"History of Wabasha County (1884), Greenfield Township: 'In April, 1851, Isaac Cole ... located on section 22, on the south bank of the Zumbro. He established a ferry and hotel and was largely patronized, for travel from Wabasha westward naturally followed the valley of the Zumbro.' Sioux camped by the hundreds along the river nearby.","t":"A hotel at a ferry on the main road west from Wabasha. Find where the Zumbro crosses section 22 and look for a house pad on the south bank above flood level. Private land: ask first.","src":[["History of Wabasha County (1884)","https://archive.org/details/historyofwabasha00hhhi"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the centre of section 22 (BLM PLSS); the site is on the south bank of the Zumbro inside it","township from the chapter heading (Greenfield)"]},{"id":"rl-johnsons-point-woodyard","L":"history","n":"Johnson's Point wood yard below Bunnell's Landing, Homer (1852)","la":44.015,"lo":-91.548,"y":"1852-1860s","co":"Winona (MN)","stt":"MN","p":"approx","pl":"Just below (downriver of) Bunnell's Landing, the upper end of Homer","v":"1850s coins, buttons, axe and chain parts","st":"History of Winona County (1883): 'Leonard Johnson came in 1852 and started up a wood-yard on a place just below Bunnell's landing called Johnson's point. John Lavine made his appearance at Minneoah, then a mushroom village just above Bunnell's landing, in 1853.' Bunnell's 1849 house still stands in Homer.","t":"Find the point on the earliest topo south-east of the Bunnell House; check whether the railroad and Highway 61 took it.","src":[["History of Winona County (1883)","https://archive.org/details/historyofwinonao00chic"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["estimate below Homer, good to about 1 km"]},{"id":"rl-jarretts-ford","L":"history","n":"Jarrett's Ford on the Zumbro (1850s crossing)","la":44.23607,"lo":-92.33906,"y":"1850s-1870s","co":"Wabasha (MN)","stt":"MN","p":"approx","pl":"Jarrett, Hyde Park Township; the ford was the nearby Zumbro crossing","v":"Early coins and wagon hardware dropped at the crossing","st":"History of Wabasha County (1920): 'there was a ford in the Zumbro where the old settlers in an early day crossed, and since the nearest dweller was Mr. Jarrett, the place came to be called Jarrett's Ford,' and the post office became Jarretts. Upham (1920): 'near a former crossing of Zumbro river, called Jarrett's Ford.' GNIS places Jarrett hamlet and the Jarretts post office.","t":"Move the pin to the old crossing on the earliest topo; the approaches on both banks are the targets.","src":[["History of Wabasha County (Curtiss-Wedge, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/historyofwabasha00curt"],["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"],["USGS GNIS: Jarrett (Millville quad)","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the hamlet (GNIS); the ford is nearby on the Zumbro"]},{"id":"rl-trempealeau-ferry-1857","L":"history","n":"Trempealeau horse ferry and 1857 steamboat dock","la":44.0055,"lo":-91.4421,"y":"1857-1880s","co":"Trempealeau","stt":"WI","p":"approx","pl":"Trempealeau village riverfront","v":"Coins 1850s on, tokens, harness parts","st":"History of Northern Wisconsin (1881): Wilson Johnston 'established the first ferry from Trempealeau Village to the Minnesota shore' in 1857; another settler 'built the first steamboat dock at Trempealeau, which is still in use as the main landing'. History of Trempealeau County (1917): the horse-tread ferry carried prairie schooners for a week at a time to the road up Pickwick Valley.","t":"The village riverfront is built up and partly park; detect only where allowed.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881)","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"],["History of Trempealeau County (Curtiss-Wedge, 1917)","https://archive.org/details/historyoftrempea00curt"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the village point (GNIS)"]},{"id":"rl-cf-frenchtown-ferry","L":"history","n":"Chippewa Falls to Frenchtown ferry, foot of Bridge Street (1857-1868)","la":44.9367,"lo":-91.3928,"y":"1857-1868","co":"Chippewa","stt":"WI","p":"approx","pl":"Foot of Bridge Street, Chippewa Falls, to Frenchtown on the south bank","v":"1850s-60s coins","st":"Chippewa County (1913): 'About the year 1857, a ferry was established between the village of Chippewa Falls and Frenchtown, now South Chippewa Falls. Passengers were taken aboard at the foot of Bridge street.' Two Frenchmen ran it, then Al Taylor until the first bridge in 1868. John Klatt was ferryman in 1862 (Randall 1875).","t":"Downtown riverfront, heavily rebuilt after the 1884 flood. Low odds; included for the record.","src":[["Chippewa County, Wisconsin, past and present (1913), vol. 1","https://archive.org/details/chippewacountywi11unse"],["Randall, History of the Chippewa Valley (1875)","https://archive.org/details/historyofchippew00rand"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["foot of Bridge Street, next to the 1884 flood pin"]},{"id":"rl-diamond-bluff-woodyard","L":"history","n":"Wethering & Niles steamboat wood yard, Diamond Bluff (1870s)","la":44.6505,"lo":-92.6291,"y":"1869-1880s","co":"Pierce","stt":"WI","p":"approx","pl":"Diamond Bluff village landing","v":"Coins, tokens, chain and axe parts","st":"History of Northern Wisconsin (1881), Diamond Bluff: 'It also has an extensive wood yard, operated by the above firm [Wethering & Niles], which supplies wood for a great many of the boats on the Mississippi.' Another settler started a wood yard in the village in 1869.","t":"The landing area below the village; the railroad runs along the bank.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881)","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["pin is the village point (GNIS)"]},{"id":"rl-stockholm-ferry-landing","L":"history","n":"Lake City-Stockholm steam ferry, Wisconsin landing (1870s-1881)","la":44.4836,"lo":-92.2652,"y":"1870s-1881","co":"Pepin","stt":"WI","p":"approx","pl":"Stockholm village shore","v":"Coins 1870s-80s, tokens","st":"History of Wabasha County (1884), Lake City chapter: the Lake City Ferry and Transportation Company ran teams and passengers to Stockholm on the Wisconsin shore; after four losing years the ferry steamer Clipper sank and 'this was the last of the attempt to maintain a regular ferry at this point for the crossing of teams and passengers between Lake City and Stockholm.'","t":"The Stockholm shoreline and the road down to it.","src":[["History of Wabasha County (1884)","https://archive.org/details/historyofwabasha00hhhi"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["Stockholm shore estimate"]},{"id":"rl-koehler-ford","L":"history","n":"Koehler Ford (old crossing named on the topo)","la":44.7382939,"lo":-90.8948704,"y":"before 1960s","co":"Clark","stt":"WI","p":"topo","pl":"Horse Creek quad, Clark County","v":"Wagon hardware, coins dropped at the crossing","st":"USGS GNIS lists Koehler Ford as a crossing (Horse Creek quad). No written history found yet.","t":"Check the approaches on both banks.","src":[["USGS GNIS: Koehler Ford (Horse Creek quad)","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["GNIS crossing point"]},{"id":"rl-smith-landing","L":"history","n":"Smith Landing on the Mississippi (named on the topo)","la":44.6891342,"lo":-92.6918644,"y":"unknown","co":"Pierce","stt":"WI","p":"topo","pl":"Between Diamond Bluff and Prescott","v":"River-landing losses","st":"USGS GNIS lists Smith Landing as a populated place (Diamond Bluff West quad). No history found yet in the 1881 or 1909 histories.","t":"Look for the landing road on LiDAR.","src":[["USGS GNIS: Smith Landing (Diamond Bluff West quad)","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["GNIS point"]},{"id":"rl-winters-landing","L":"history","n":"Winters Landing on the Mississippi (named on the topo)","la":43.9416304,"lo":-91.3668091,"y":"unknown","co":"La Crosse","stt":"WI","p":"topo","pl":"Holmen quad, Mississippi bottoms","v":"River-landing losses","st":"USGS GNIS lists Winters Landing (Holmen quad) in both the Wisconsin and Minnesota files. No history found yet.","t":"Probably refuge bottomland: check the Land box.","src":[["USGS GNIS: Winters Landing (Holmen quad)","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"c":"documented","s":"unknown","pinconf":"C","pinev":["GNIS point"]}]);
{ const E={"vs-1843090":{"st":" History of Vernon County (1884): John Warner settled at Warner's Landing in 1848; it is on section 4, T13N R7W, one of three 'good landings on the Mississippi' in the county (with Britt's Landing, sec. 9, and Bad Ax City, sec. 28). The GNIS point falls inside section 4, so two kinds of source agree.","src":[["History of Vernon County (Springer, 1884)","https://archive.org/details/historyofvernonc00spri"]]},"pbp77-steam-ferry-at-durand-sec-21-t25n-r13w":{"st":" History of Northern Wisconsin (1881), Durand: Jacob Kuhn and John Schell started a pole ferry across the Chippewa in 1857; later owners were Stokes (pole ferry), Sol Crosby (horse ferry), Babcock and the Goodrich brothers; H. Rollin Smith bought an interest in 1870. The Durand steamboat landing card (ld-durand-landing) covers the levee.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881)","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]},"pp77-red-wing-ferry-wisconsin-landing-sec-10-":{"st":" History of Goodhue County (1909): the first crossing was a horse ferry between Red Wing and Trenton run by Capt. Edward Speck; a second ferry worked the Wisconsin channel; later the Red Wing & Trenton Transit Company ran a ferry and a road across the island, leased to J. W. Day to 1884 and W. K. Hawkins 1884-86.","src":[["History of Goodhue County (Curtiss-Wedge, 1909)","https://archive.org/details/historyofgoodhue00curt"]]}}; addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{ for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; if(x.src.length) p.src=[...(p.src||[]),...x.src]; } }); }

// County-depth card notes: hathi-batch1-enrich.json
{ const E={"pc88-twelve-mile-house-sec-7-t30n-r8w": {"st": " Second source, History of Northern Wisconsin (1881, p. 201): Twelve-Mile House is the second stop on the stage road from Chippewa Falls to Big Bend and the Chippewa pinery, after Nine-Mile House. The 1881 list then runs Campbell's (10 miles on), Larrabee House (+3), Lake House (+1) and Big Bend (+9).", "src": [["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road", "https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]}, "pc88-campbell-house-sec-1-t31n-r9w": {"st": " Second source, History of Northern Wisconsin (1881, p. 201): 'Campbell's' is the stop 10 miles past Twelve-Mile House on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend stage road, with Larrabee House 3 miles on.", "src": [["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road", "https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]}, "pc88-lake-house-on-long-lake-sec-18-t32n-r8w": {"st": " Second source, History of Northern Wisconsin (1881, p. 201): the Lake House is the stop 1 mile past Larrabee House and 9 miles short of Big Bend on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend stage road, about 26 road miles from Chippewa Falls. (Not the Lake House on Cornell Lake, about 13 miles out.)", "src": [["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road", "https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]}, "pb13-eagleton-hotel": {"st": " Possible earlier use: the 1881 History of Northern Wisconsin (p. 201) lists Nine-Mile House, with a post office, as the first stop on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend stage road. The Eagleton corner is about 9 miles by road north of Chippewa Falls and 3 miles short of the Twelve-Mile House pin, so this hotel may stand on or near the Nine-Mile House site. This is not proved.", "src": [["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road", "https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]}, "ab-hall-raynor-stop": {"st": " The 1881 History of Northern Wisconsin (p. 201) names a 'Hall House' among the stopping places on the road north from Chippewa Falls by way of Big Bend, which fits this Hall stopping place on the Chippewa Trail.", "src": [["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road", "https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"]]}, "pbu15-falun-sec-18-t38n-r17w": {"st": " Burnett County history (Peet, 1902): Falun had a woolen and carding mill and a tannery in its early years, besides the store and post office.", "src": [["History of Burnett County (Rev. Emerson W. Peet, 1902), via HathiTrust", "https://catalog.hathitrust.org/"]]}}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=E[p.id]; if(!x) continue; if(x.fix) p.st=(p.st||"").replace(x.fix[0],x.fix[1]); p.st=(p.st||"")+x.st; (p.src=p.src||[]).push(...x.src); } }

// County-depth new pins: hathi-batch1-new-pins.json
DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"cd-larrabee-house","L":"history","n":"Larrabee House: stage stop by Larrabee Lake","la":45.2425,"lo":-91.428,"y":"1870s-1880s","pl":"Chippewa County, between Campbell House (sec. 1 T31N R9W) and the Lake House on Long Lake (sec. 18 T32N R8W), by Larrabee Lake","co":"Chippewa","stt":"WI","v":"Coins 1860s-1880s, stage and teamster losses, harness, bottles","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"History of Northern Wisconsin (1881, p. 201) lists the stopping places on the stage road from Chippewa Falls to Big Bend: Nine-Mile House, Twelve-Mile House, Campbell's (+10 miles), Larrabee House (+3), Lake House (+1), Big Bend (+9), and on north. Campbell's and the Lake House are both on the 1888 plat (J. E. Campbell owned both). Larrabee House falls between them, and Larrabee Lake (GNIS) sits right there, which is where the stop took its name.","t":"Look along the old road line between Campbell House and the Long Lake shore for a yard or cellar near Larrabee Lake, using the 1888 plat road and a 1938 air photo. Ask first.","src":[["History of Northern Wisconsin (Western Historical Co., 1881), p. 201, stopping places on the Chippewa Falls-Big Bend road","https://archive.org/details/historyofnorther00west"],["USGS GNIS: Larrabee Lake, Chippewa Co.","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"],["Plat Book of Chippewa County (C. M. Foote, 1888): Campbell House and Lake House","https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/record/maps/W017391/x"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Placed from the 1881 mileages between two 1888 plat stops, next to GNIS Larrabee Lake (45.2441,-91.4317). The building itself is not found; it could be a few hundred meters off."]},{"id":"cd-branstad-creamery","L":"history","n":"Branstad: the vanished settlement and 1897 creamery south of Grantsburg","la":45.7375,"lo":-92.683,"y":"1890s-1900s","pl":"Branstad, about 2 miles south of Grantsburg (GNIS point)","co":"Burnett","stt":"WI","v":"Coins 1890s-1910s, creamery tokens, farm and store losses","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Peet's History of Burnett County (1902) lists Branstad, about 2 miles south of Grantsburg, among the county's small post-office centers, with a creamery built in 1897. GNIS still keeps the Branstad name at this point. The nearest existing pin is the Bethany Church site about 1 km away.","t":"Find the creamery and store on the 1915 plat and a 1938 air photo; creamery yards got daily farm traffic. Ask first.","src":[["History of Burnett County (Rev. Emerson W. Peet, 1902), via HathiTrust","https://catalog.hathitrust.org/"],["USGS GNIS: Branstad, Burnett Co.","https://edits.nationalmap.gov/apps/gaz-domestic/public/search/names"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Pin is the GNIS Branstad point; the creamery building is not placed."]}]);

