// Fur-trade posts (research/fur-trade)
DATA.points.push(...[{"id":"fur-grey-cloud-island","L":"history","n":"Grey Cloud Island trading posts (Joseph R. Brown, Mooers and Robertson, 1838-47)","la":44.806,"lo":-93.008,"y":"1838-1847","pl":"West end of Upper Grey Cloud Island; Brown's log house was farther east, down the river","co":"Washington","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Hazen Mooers, Andrew Robertson and Joseph R. Brown came here together from Little Rock in 1838. Mooers and Robertson took three big bark lodges on the west end of the island, left the autumn before by Medicine Bottle's band; Brown built a log house farther east and then a second post, trading west through branch houses (Case 1915). Robertson named the island for his mother-in-law, Margaret Aird Mooers (Grey Cloud).","t":"Much of the island is gravel quarry or private land: get permission. Brown's house site east of the pin is not fixed.","src":[["Case, \"Historical Notes of Grey Cloud Island,\" MHS Collections XV (1915) 371-378","https://archive.org/details/collectionsofmin0015unse"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Case 1915 (eyewitness family source) and Nute 1930 no. 115 agree on Grey Cloud Island","GNIS Upper Grey Cloud Island 44.80469, -92.99688; pin moved to the island's west end per Case"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-sunrise-samuels","L":"history","n":"Maurice M. Samuels's trading post, opposite Sunrise Island (1846)","la":45.5615,"lo":-92.86,"y":"1846","pl":"West bank of the St. Croix a little below the mouth of the Sunrise River, in front of Sunrise Island","co":"Chisago","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Folsom passed it in 1847: 'In front of Sunrise island and on the west side of the St. Croix river, a little below the mouth of Sunrise river, stood the trading post of Maurice M. Samuels, long known as one of the most remarkable and notorious men on the frontier.' Samuels married a Chippewa woman. Nute 1930 (no. 87) cites only Folsom.","t":"Riverway shore: check whether the bank is National Park Service land before detecting.","src":[["Folsom, Fifty Years in the Northwest (1888)","https://archive.org/details/fiftyyearsinnort00folsuoft"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Folsom's landmark description; Nute repeats it (not independent)","GNIS Sunrise River mouth 45.56663, -92.86271; pin a short way downstream on the west bank","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur","acc":["warn","Riverway shore may be National Park Service land (no detecting); check the owner first"]},{"id":"fur-rust-groundhouse","L":"history","n":"Henry Rust's trading post, Groundhouse River (mid-1840s; Rust murdered 1847)","la":45.8,"lo":-93.298,"y":"about 1845-1847","pl":"About two miles above the mouth of the Groundhouse River, a western branch of the Snake","co":"Kanabec","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Henry Rust kept a post in the middle 1840s about two miles above the mouth of Ground House River (Nute, from Folsom). Folsom gives the 1847 murder of Rust and the trial of two Ojibwe men at Stillwater, who were acquitted.","t":"Farm and woods along the river: get permission. The spot is only measured from the river mouth.","src":[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["Folsom, Fifty Years in the Northwest (1888)","https://archive.org/details/fiftyyearsinnort00folsuoft"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Nute 1930 no. 79 from Folsom (one source line)","GNIS Groundhouse River mouth 45.81079, -93.26217; pin about 3 km upstream","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-porlier-crow-river","L":"history","n":"Porlier's wintering post, mouth of the Crow River (1797-1806)","la":45.2445,"lo":-93.518,"y":"1797-1806","pl":"Island at the mouth of the Crow River, near Dayton","co":"Hennepin / Wright","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Jacques Porlier's post was at the mouth of the Crow River near Dayton, apparently on an island; Pike stopped there in 1805-06. Porlier had wintered here in 1797. Robert Dickson's wintering post was some miles farther up the Mississippi (WHC XIX note; Nute no. 83 puts Dickson just below Sauk Rapids).","t":"Islands and banks here are public water or park land in places: check the owner.","src":[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Editor's note in WHC XIX from Pike's journal","GNIS Crow River mouth 45.24580, -93.52246","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-cannon-mouth-fort-la-pointe","L":"history","n":"'Fort La Pointe': Charles Patterson's sub-post at the mouth of the Cannon River (1786)","la":44.5858,"lo":-92.5568,"y":"1786","pl":"Mouth of the Cannon River on the Mississippi, west of Red Wing","co":"Goodhue","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"In 1786 Charles Patterson had a sub-post at the mouth of the Cannon River; it appears as 'Fort La Pointe' on Perrault's map (Nute no. 122, citing Perrault's Narrative, Michigan Pioneer and Historical Collections 37: 539).","t":"Bottomland and wildlife refuge around the mouth: check the owner.","src":[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Nute 1930 from Perrault's map","GNIS Cannon River mouth 44.58580, -92.55686","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-sturgeon-lake-stockade","L":"restricted","n":"Stockaded fort of nine buildings, Sturgeon Lake (found 1885; possibly Marin's 1750 post)","la":44.6367,"lo":-92.6414,"y":"1700s?","pl":"Section 32, T114N R15W, on the shore of Sturgeon Lake, Prairie Island","co":"Goodhue","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"plss","st":"In 1885 the Hill-Lewis survey found the remains of a large stockaded fort of some nine buildings in section 32 of township 114, range 15 W., on Sturgeon Lake. Nute thought its size suggested a French post, possibly Marin's post of 1750, which has never been located.","t":"Prairie Island Indian Community and plant land: no access or detecting without permission. Look only; report anything to the State Archaeologist.","src":[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["BLM PLSS CadNSDI section outlines","https://gis.blm.gov/arcgis/rest/services/Cadastral/BLM_Natl_PLSS_CadNSDI/MapServer"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Nute 1930 no. 117a from the Hill-Lewis notebooks","Pin is the centre of sec. 32 T114N R15W (BLM)","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur","acc":["stop","Tribal land: no detecting without the tribe's written permission; burials nearby"]},{"id":"fur-fort-le-sueur","L":"restricted","n":"Fort Le Sueur (1695), Prairie Island","la":44.6491,"lo":-92.6913,"y":"1695","pl":"Prairie Island (Isle Pelee), Mississippi above Red Wing","co":"Goodhue","stt":"MN","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Pierre Charles le Sueur built a post in 1695, probably on Isle Pelee, now Prairie Island (Nute no. 117). Radisson and Groseilliers had landed here in 1655-56 (Upham).","t":"Prairie Island Indian Community and wetland: look only.","src":[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (MHS Collections XVII, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Nute and Upham name the island, not a spot on it","GNIS Prairie Island 44.64913, -92.69131"],"flag":"fur","acc":["stop","Tribal land: no detecting without the tribe's written permission; burials nearby"]},{"id":"fur-superior-old-fort","L":"history","n":"'Old Fort': Cadotte and North West Company post at the base of Connors Point, Superior (about 1790-1816)","la":46.7315,"lo":-92.0985,"y":"about 1790-1816","pl":"Base of Connors Point near the old city gas works, Superior","co":"Douglas","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"Jean Baptiste Cadotte II opened the Fond du Lac department for the North West Company about 1790 and built a permanent post on the bay, where two Indians were executed in 1796. Local tradition called it 'old fort'; it was at the base of Connor's Point, not far from the gas works of Superior (James Bardon in WHC XIX). Schoolcraft and Doty saw its remains in 1820, abandoned about six years. The 1925 Record says the American Fur Company post stood where the city gas plant is. It was the supply depot for the whole Fond du Lac department, on the portage between Superior and St. Louis bays.","t":"Industrial waterfront; the old gas works ground is a coal-tar cleanup site (C Street Slip): do not dig there. Look only unless an owner allows it.","src":[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"],["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["WHC XIX (Bardon, with Schoolcraft 1820) and Wisconsin Archeologist 1925 agree on the base of Connors Point by the gas works","GNIS Connors Point 46.74661, -92.10019 is the tip; pin at the base about 1.7 km SE, within about 500 m"],"flag":"fur","acc":["warn","Former gas-works ground is a contaminated cleanup site: do not dig"]},{"id":"fur-trade-lake-post","L":"history","n":"Trade Lake trading post ('Hudson Bay Co.'), between the Trade lakes","la":45.6758,"lo":-92.607,"y":"fur trade, before 1850s","pl":"Sec. 21, T37N R18W, at the thoroughfare between Big and Little Trade lakes","co":"Burnett","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"plss","st":"L. E. Thomas (1913) wrote that Trade Lake 'probably obtained its name from the fact that it was the trading post for the country south of the Yellow river, as far south as Osceola, St. Croix Falls, Stillwater and Hudson'. The 1925 Record lists a 'Hudson Bay Co.' trading post between the Trade lakes in section 21. A trail ran from Cavillion's post at Yellow Lake to Trade Lake and on to St. Croix Falls.","t":"Lakeshore lots: owner permission. The 'Hudson Bay' name is local tradition; it was probably a North West or American Fur Company house.","src":[["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.13 no.1 (1913), Brown, northwestern Wisconsin notes (Yellow Lake and Trade Lake posts)","https://archive.org/details/wisconsinarcheol13wiscrich"],["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["BLM PLSS CadNSDI section outlines","https://gis.blm.gov/arcgis/rest/services/Cadastral/BLM_Natl_PLSS_CadNSDI/MapServer"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Both records go back to Thomas's 1913 letter (one source)","Sec. 21 T37N R18W (BLM) holds Little Trade Lake; pin at the narrows to Big Trade Lake (GNIS lakes 45.68023, -92.60102 and 45.67041, -92.61696)","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-shell-lake-island","L":"history","n":"Trading post on the middle island, Shell Lake","la":45.7263,"lo":-91.911,"y":"fur trade","pl":"Island in Shell Lake (probably today's Scout Island)","co":"Washburn","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"approx","st":"C. E. Brown (1913): the Chippewa formerly camped about Shell Lake, and a trading post was on Middle island in the lake. Mounds and a camp stood on the north shore.","t":"Check who owns the island. Mounds on the north shore: look only there.","src":[["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["One record (Brown 1913, repeated 1925)","Pin is GNIS Scout Island 45.72634, -91.91101, the main island near the middle of the lake","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-vance-hay-river","L":"history","n":"Peter Vance's trading post, Hay River (Vanceburg)","la":45.189,"lo":-92.0035,"y":"1850s-60s","pl":"West bank of the Hay River, NW 1/4 sec. 8, T31N R13W (Sheridan town), at Vanceburg","co":"Dunn","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"plss","st":"The 1925 Record: 'Peter Vance trading post was on the west bank of Hay river, N. W. 1/4 Sec. 8' in Sheridan township (J. A. H. Johnson). The 1888 Dunn plat shows the village of Vanceburgh, its mill, dam and pond in the same quarter.","t":"Trade-era losses here are trade silver, glass beads, gun flints and gun parts, kettle brass, lead seals and buttons. Record what you find and leave anything Native American in place. Get the owner's permission; the mill and dam ground is next to the post site.","src":[["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["Plat Book of Dunn County, Wisconsin (C. M. Foote & Co., 1888), Vanceburgh","https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/record/map/W017R7J"],["BLM PLSS CadNSDI section outlines","https://gis.blm.gov/arcgis/rest/services/Cadastral/BLM_Natl_PLSS_CadNSDI/MapServer"]],"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Record says NW 1/4 sec. 8 on the west bank; 1888 plat Vanceburgh sits in SE NW sec. 8 T31N R13W (BLM GetTRS) on the Hay River","Pin between the plat village pin and the mill dam, on the west bank"],"flag":"fur"},{"id":"fur-trout-lake-wright","L":"restricted","n":"Old trading post and village site, Wright place, west shore of Trout Lake","la":46.036,"lo":-89.706,"y":"1700s-1840s","pl":"NE 1/4 sec. 14, T41N R6E, west shore of Trout Lake","co":"Vilas","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"plss","st":"The 1925 Record (G. A. West): a village site, the site of an old trading post and a group of seven oblong and conical mounds on the Wright place on the west shore of Trout Lake, NE 1/4 sec. 14. Malhiot's 1804-05 journal names Lac de la Truite as the village of the chief L'Outarde and sends goods and rum there all winter; the editor notes the Chippewa stopped at Trout Lake before moving to Lac du Flambeau.","t":"Mounds here: look only, never dig. Much of the shore is state forest or private: check the owner.","src":[["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"],["BLM PLSS CadNSDI section outlines","https://gis.blm.gov/arcgis/rest/services/Cadastral/BLM_Natl_PLSS_CadNSDI/MapServer"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["Section from the 1925 Record; Malhiot confirms the trading village at the lake but not the spot","Pin in NE 1/4 sec. 14 T41N R6E (BLM), set toward the west shore","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur","acc":["stop","Archaeological site, burial ground, park or tribal land: look only, never dig or detect"]},{"id":"fur-cadotte-chippewa-falls","L":"history","n":"Michel Cadotte's trading post above Chippewa Falls (about 1791)","la":44.942,"lo":-91.39,"y":"about 1784-1800","pl":"On the Chippewa River just above the falls","co":"Chippewa","stt":"WI","v":"Fur-trade post: trade silver, beads, gun parts, kettle brass, lead bale seals","c":"documented","s":"unknown","p":"area","st":"Thwaites (WHC XIX) notes that Michel Cadotte Jr. 'was born at his father's trading post on Chippewa River, just above Chippewa Falls, in 1791'. By 1784 the elder Cadotte was wintering at the head of the Chippewa. In 1843 Alfred Brunson met the younger Cadotte at his brother-in-law's post on the Chippewa.","t":"The ground above the falls is now the flowage and city: this is history, not a dig. Any river-bank lot needs the owner's permission.","src":[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"]],"pinconf":"C","pinev":["One source (editor's note, WHC XIX)","Pin is a general spot above the falls; the site is not known","Unconfirmed, one source","Unconfirmed, one source"],"flag":"fur"}]);
{ const F={"fr-mendota-st-peter-s-trader-settlement-and-f":[{"acc":["stop","State park / historic site land: metal detecting is not allowed"]}," Fur trade: After J. B. Faribault's post on Pike Island (1818-20), the American Fur Company set its depot for the whole Sioux trade on the shore at Mendota. Henry H. Sibley built the stone house in 1836; Faribault had built the first stone house in Minnesota here after 1826 (Upham). Nute lists it as the general depot for trade with the Sioux.",[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (MHS Collections XVII, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]]],"fr-pike-island-treaty-ground-1805":[{"acc":["stop","State park / historic site land: metal detecting is not allowed"]}," Fur trade: In 1810 Allen Wilmot, Thomas G. Anderson and Joseph Rolette built a stockaded fort on Pike's Island, the earliest recorded post at the mouth of the St. Peter's (Minnesota) River. Jean Baptiste Faribault moved his post here in 1818-20 under the troops' protection, before going to Mendota (Upham). Pike signed his 1805 treaty on the west end of the island.",[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (MHS Collections XVII, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"],["Anderson, Personal Narrative, Wisconsin Historical Collections IX (1882) 179","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate09stat_0"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]]],"fr-land-s-end-minnesota-river-bluff":[{"acc":["warn","Fort Snelling reservation land today is state park, airport or federal: check the owner first"]}," Fur trade: Land's End was a trading post about a mile above Fort Snelling on the Minnesota River, kept by Joseph R. Brown in 1831 (Nute). On the 1839 Fort Snelling reservation map 'Land's End' is the part of the northwest bluff nearly two miles southwest of the fort where a ravine intersects it (Upham).",[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (MHS Collections XVII, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"]]],"d-reads-landing":[{}," Fur trade: Upham: Read's Landing 'is on the site occupied as a Sioux trading post by Augustine Rocque from about 1810 to 1825 or 1830; by his son bearing the same name, from 1835 till his death, about 1860; by an Englishman named Hudson, from 1840 till he died, in 1845; and by Charles R. Read, who came here in 1847.' Nute adds that the elder Rocque's post, set up during the Revolution to win Sioux help against the Spanish at St. Louis, was apparently on this site; the younger Rocque traded first opposite the Chippewa mouth and later on Beef Slough (Buffalo County, WI). Alexis Bailly and Joseph La Bathe later traded at Wabasha.",[["Upham, Minnesota Geographic Names (MHS Collections XVII, 1920)","https://archive.org/details/minnesotageograp00upharich"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"],["USGS GNIS Domestic Names (2024 file in project folder)","https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis"]]],"t-fort-st-antoine":[{"pinconf":"B","p":"plss","acc":["warn","If the stone pile or embankments show, this is an archaeological site: look and report, do not dig"],"pinev":["Draper 1888 (Miller's 1855 survey) and Nute 1930 agree on sec. 20 T23N R15W above the Bogus Creek mouth","BLM sec. 20 box 44.4588-44.4673 N; GNIS Bogus Creek mouth 44.45469, -92.21934; pin about 680 m up the shore from the mouth","Move from the old Stockholm guess about 4 km"],"la":44.4592,"lo":-92.2248}," In 1855 surveyor Miller, laying out Pepin and Stockholm, was shown 'the old French fort' on the lake shore in section 20, T23N R15W, about 130-140 rods above the mouth of Bogus Creek: nearly rectangular lines 1-2 ft high, with a large pile of stone at the south-west angle (Draper 1888). Settlers at the first settlement saw vestiges 'a little above the mouth of Bogus creek'; the plow had nearly erased them by 1888. Nute (1930) gives the same section.",[["Draper, \"Early French Forts in Western Wisconsin,\" Wisconsin Historical Collections X (1888) 366-372","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate10stat_0"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"]]],"wd3-cavillion":[{"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Thomas 1913 and Peck 1911 independently put the post at the Yellow Lake thoroughfare in sec. 23 T40N R17W","Peck says about 40 rods (200 m) south of the thoroughfare, on Big Yellow Lake: Ground check, nudge the pin there"],"dla":-0.0018}," B. F. Peck of Spooner told the WHC editors (1911) the post 'was located on Big Yellow Lake, about forty rods south of the thoroughfare between that and Little Yellow Lake in section 23 of township 40 north, 17 west'. Nute (1930) lists Yellow Lake as an American Fur Company fort of the 1830s, for years in charge of Dr. Charles W. W. Borup. Curot's 1803-04 X Y post on the Yellow River was elsewhere (see Forts Folle Avoine).",[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XX (1911): Curot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate20stat"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"]]],"gs-montra-house-1854":[{"acc":["warn","Mounds on Leinenkugel Point: look only around them"],"pinev":["1854 survey house, 1925 Record and Barron 1922 (Beagle) agree on the north shore of Lake Chetek","Leinenkugel Point itself is partly under water since the dam: Ground check, compare the 1854 plat shoreline"]}," American Fur Company post on the north bank of Lake Chetek, built by Joseph Trepanier in 1836 and later held by Louis Montra (1925 Record). Daniel Beagle, an early Chetek settler, said the post stood in 1830-40 on Leinenkugel Point near the later Red Club House, and the trader's log cabin still stood in 1865 (Barron 1922). Kellogg thought Montra was an employee of Jean Baptiste Corbin of Lac Courte Oreilles. The point had three mounds and a Chippewa camp; the dam has drowned and eroded much of it.",[["The Wisconsin Archeologist v.4 n.s. (1925), Fifth Addition to the Record of Wisconsin Antiquities","https://archive.org/details/archeolwisconsin04wiscrich"],["Curtiss-Wedge, History of Barron County (1922), ch. IV and the mound survey","https://archive.org/details/wi-barron-1922-curtiss-wedge-1"]]],"ab-sandy-lake-posts":[{"pinev":["NWC site is site 21AK04 (burials): left unpinned on purpose"]}," The 1794 North West Company post on the west shore is Minnesota site 21AK04; human remains came out of its eroding bank in 1966, and the American Fur Company post at the outlet is part of site 21AK11 inside the Corps of Engineers recreation area. Nute (1930) gives both sites (west side of the lake; near the Sandy-Mississippi junction).",[["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"]]],"tg-lac-du-flambeau-posts":[{"pinev":["Malhiot's fort site is unknown (WHC XIX note); pin stays approximate"]}," François Malhiot built the North West Company's fort here in 1804-05; he wrote on April 17, 1805 that its pickets were planted and 'it is the finest of all the savage departments'. The rival X Y Company post was near by. The WHC editors (1910) found no data fixing either site. Later traders: Roderick Lawrence (American Fur Co. clerk, 1818) and Truman Warren (1820s).",[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"]]],"tg-namekagon-portage":[{"pinev":[]}," Jean Baptiste Corbin was sent by Michel Cadotte in 1800 to open a post on Lac Courte Oreilles; it was plundered in 1808, and Corbin lived there the rest of his life, joining the American Fur Company in 1818 (WHC XIX note). Its exact site is not recorded.",[["Wisconsin Historical Collections XIX (1910): Malhiot journal and editor's notes","https://archive.org/details/collectionsstate19stat"]]],"ob-frontenac-sandy-point":[{"pinconf":"B","pinev":["Neill 1887, Pike 1806 (in WHC X) and Nute 1930 agree on Sandy Point opposite Maiden Rock; pin stays on the point (within about 500 m)"]}," Neill (1887): on September 17, 1727 La Perriere chose 'a low point, about the middle of the lake shore, opposite Maiden's Rock' and built a stockade 100 feet square with two bastions, a commandant's house, a house for the Jesuits and a store; after floods it was rebuilt on higher ground near by in 1731. Nute (1930) adds that in 1830 a trader, probably of the American Fur Company, still had a post on Point des Sables.",[["Neill, The Last French Post in the Valley of the Upper Mississippi, near Frontenac (1887)","https://archive.org/details/lastfrenchpostin00neil"],["Nute, \"Posts in the Minnesota Fur-Trading Area, 1660-1855,\" Minnesota History 11 (1930) 353-385","https://collections.mnhs.org/MNHistoryMagazine/articles/11/v11i04p353-385.pdf"]]]}; for(const p of DATA.points){ const x=F[p.id]; if(!x) continue; const o=Object.assign({},x[0]); if(o.dla){ o.la=+(p.la+o.dla).toFixed(5); delete o.dla; } const mv=o.la!=null&&(o.la!==p.la||o.lo!=null&&o.lo!==p.lo); Object.assign(p,o); if(mv){ p.st=(p.st||"").replace(/ Air photo check \(UW[^)]*\):[^.]*\./g,"").replace(/ LiDAR cache check[^.]*\.[^.]*\./g,""); delete LIDC[p.id]; delete LIDIMG[p.id]; } p.st=(p.st||"")+x[1]; if(x[2].length) p.src=[...(p.src||[]),...x[2]]; } }

